"Angular constraints on planar frameworks | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2403.16145)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; In this setting we provide a matricial rigidity formulation of the problem for colored graphs, an algebro-geometric reformulation, precise necessary conditions and a combinatorial characterization of the generic behaviour for a special case. ... @misc{dewar2026angularconstraintsplanarframeworks, title={Angular constraints on planar frameworks}, author={Sean Dewar and Georg Grasegger and Anthony Nixon and Zvi Rosen and William Sims and Meera Sitharam and David Urizar}, year={2026}, eprint={2403.16145}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.16145}, }\nSubmitted 24 Mar 2024\n\n# Angular constraints on planar frameworks\n\nSD\n\nSean Dewar\n\nGG\n\nGeorg Grasegger\n\nAN\n\nAnthony Nixon\n\nZR\n\nZvi Rosen\n\nWS\n\nWilliam Sims\n\nMS\n\nMeera Sitharam\n\nDU\n\nDavid Urizar\n\n## Abstract\n\nConsider a collection of points and the sets of slopes or directions of the lines between pairs of points. It is known that the algebraic matroid on this set of elements is the well studied 2-dimensional rigidity matroid. This article analyzes a construction on top of the set of slopes given by an angle constraint system of incidences and angles. In this setting we provide a matricial rigidity formulation of the problem for colored graphs, an algebro-geometric reformulation, precise necessary conditions and a combinatorial characterization of the generic behaviour for a special case.\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{dewar2026angularconstraintsplanarframeworks, title={Angular constraints on planar frameworks}, author={Sean Dewar and Georg Grasegger and Anthony Nixon and Zvi Rosen and William Sims and Meera Sitharam and David Urizar}, year={2026}, eprint={2403.16145}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.16145}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAngular constraints on planar frameworks - Lancaster University research directory (https://research.lancaster-university.uk/en/publications/angular-constraints-on-planar-frameworks/)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; We formulate the problem in matricial terms for certain edge-colored graphs, finding precise necessary conditions for when such edge-colored graphs are rigid, and a combinatorial characterization of generic rigidity for a special case. ...   * 2403.16145v2 Accepted author manuscript, 600 KB Licence: CC BY: Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License\n\n  * https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.16145\n\n  * https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0166218X26001514 ... Engineering and Physical Sciences Research Council\n\n## Access to Document\n\n  * 10.1016/j.dam.2026.03.009\n\n  * 2403.16145v2Accepted author manuscript, 600 KB Licence: CC BY: Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License\n\n  * https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.16145\n\n  * https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0166218X26001514\n\n## Projects\n\n  * 1 Active\n\nProjects per year\n\n  * ### Abstract Rigidity for natural stability problems\n\nNixon, A. (Principal Investigator)\n\nEngineering and Physical Sciences Research Council\n\n1/12/23 → 31/07/27\n\nProject: Research\n\nDewar, S., Grasegger, G., Nixon, A., Rosen, Z., Sims, W., Sitharam, M., & Urizar, D. (2026). Angular constraints on planar frameworks. Discrete Applied Mathematics, 390, 151-166. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2026.03.009\n\nDewar, Sean ; Grasegger, Georg ; Nixon, Anthony et al. / Angular constraints on planar frameworks. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAngular constraints on planar frameworks | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/404217860_Angular_constraints_on_planar_frameworks)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2403.16145 ...   * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet. ... Consider a collection of points in the plane and the sets of slopes or directions of the lines between pairs of points. ... The central question we analyze is: when is an angle system rigid, in the sense that every nontrivial motion alters one of the fixed angles? We formulate the problem in matricial terms for certain edge-colored graphs, finding precise necessary conditions for when such edge-colored graphs are rigid, and a combinatorial characterization of generic rigidity for a special case.\nPreprint\n\n# Angular constraints on planar frameworks\n\n  * April 2026\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2403.16145\n\nAuthors:\n\nSean Dewar\n\n  * University of Bristol\n\nGeorg Grasegger\n\nGeorg Grasegger\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nAnthony Nixon\n\n  * Lancaster University\n\nZvi Rosen\n\nZvi Rosen\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nShow all 7 authors Hide\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nConsider a collection of points in the plane and the sets of slopes or directions of the lines between pairs of points. It is known that the algebraic matroid on the set of direction constraints between the points is equivalent to the algebraic matroid on the set of distances between the points. This is the well-studied generic 2-dimensional rigidity matroid of a graph. This article studies a higher-level construction built on the slope data: an angle constraint system obtained by prescribing relationships between pairs of slopes. The central question we analyze is: when is an angle system rigid, in the sense that every nontrivial motion alters one of the fixed angles? We formulate the problem in matricial terms for certain edge-colored graphs, finding precise necessary conditions for when such edge-colored graphs are rigid, and a combinatorial characterization of generic rigidity for a special case. We also prove the validity of an equivalent formulation of the angle matroid as the algebraic matroid of a field extension.\n\n\n## Citations (0)\n\n## References (20)\n\nResearchGate has not been able to resolve any citations for this publication.\n\nGeometry of graph varieties\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * May 2003\n  * T AM MATH SOC\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Baril_A in:cs - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3ABaril_A+in%3Acs)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago;   * The Fine-Grained Complexity of Graph Homomorphism Problems: Towards the Okrasa and Rzążewski Conjecture ... @misc{2404.09798, author = {Ambroise Baril and Miguel Couceiro and Victor Lagerkvist}, title = {{T}he {F}ine-{G}rained {C}omplexity of {G}raph {H}omomorphism {P}roblems: {T}owards the {O}krasa and {R}zążewski {C}onjecture}, year = {2024}, eprint = {2404.09798}, note = {arXiv:2404.09798v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n  * The Fine-Grained Complexity of Graph Homomorphism Problems: Towards the Okrasa and Rzążewski Conjecture\n\nAmbroise Baril, Miguel Couceiro, Victor Lagerkvist\n\nApr 16 2024 cs.CC math.CO arXiv:2404.09798v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n1\n\n@misc{2404.09798, author = {Ambroise Baril and Miguel Couceiro and Victor Lagerkvist}, title = {{T}he {F}ine-{G}rained {C}omplexity of {G}raph {H}omomorphism {P}roblems: {T}owards the {O}krasa and {R}zążewski {C}onjecture}, year = {2024}, eprint = {2404.09798}, note = {arXiv:2404.09798v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nIn this paper we are interested in the fine-grained complexity of deciding whether there is a homomorphism from an input graph $G$ to a fixed graph $H$ (the $H$-Coloring problem). The starting point is that these problems can be viewed as constraint satisfaction problems (CSPs), and that (partial) polymorphisms of binary relations are of paramount importance in the study of complexity classes of such CSPs. Thus, we first investigate the expressivity of binary symmetric relations $E_H$ and their corresponding (partial) polymorphisms pPol($E_H$). For irreflexive graphs we observe that there is no pair of graphs $H$ and $H'$ such that pPol($E_H$) $\\subseteq$ pPol($E_{H'}$), unless $E_{H'}= \\emptyset$ or $H =H'$. More generally we show the existence of an $n$-ary relation $R$ whose partial polymorphisms strictly subsume those of $H$ and such that CSP($R$) is NP-complete if and only if $H$ contains an odd cycle of length at most $n$. Motivated by this we also describe the sets of total polymorphisms of nontrivial cliques, odd cycles, as well as certain cores, and we give an algebraic characterization of projective cores. As a by-product, we settle the Okrasa and Rzążewski conjecture for all graphs of at most 7 vertices.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAngular constraints on planar frameworks — Scifaro (https://scifaro.com/en/abs/angular-constraints-on-planar-frameworks-2403.16145)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; We formulate the problem in matricial terms for certain edge-colored graphs, finding precise necessary conditions for when such edge-colored graphs are rigid, and a combinatorial characterization of generic rigidity for a special case. ...     @article{arxiv.2403.16145, ...       journal= {arXiv preprint arXiv:2403.16145},\n\nHome › Combinatorics › Angular constraints on planar frameworks\n\n# Angular constraints on planar frameworks\n\nCombinatorics 2026-04-27 v2 Metric Geometry\n\nAuthors: Sean Dewar , Georg Grasegger , Anthony Nixon , Zvi Rosen , William Sims , Meera Sitharam , David Urizar\n\nView on arXiv ↗ PDF ↗\n\n### Abstract\n\nConsider a collection of points in the plane and the sets of slopes or directions of the lines between pairs of points. It is known that the algebraic matroid on the set of direction constraints between the points is equivalent to the algebraic matroid on the set of distances between the points. This is the well-studied generic 2-dimensional rigidity matroid of a graph. This article studies a higher-level construction built on the slope data: an angle constraint system obtained by prescribing relationships between pairs of slopes. The central question we analyze is: when is an angle system rigid, in the sense that every nontrivial motion alters one of the fixed angles? We formulate the problem in matricial terms for certain edge-colored graphs, finding precise necessary conditions for when such edge-colored graphs are rigid, and a combinatorial characterization of generic rigidity for a special case. We also prove the validity of an equivalent formulation of the angle matroid as the algebraic matroid of a field extension.\n\n### Keywords\n\nplanar framework angle constraint rigidity matroid generic rigidity algebraic matroid edge-colored graph\n\n### Cite\n    \n    @article{arxiv.2403.16145,\n      title  = {Angular constraints on planar frameworks},\n      author = {Sean Dewar and Georg Grasegger and Anthony Nixon and Zvi Rosen and William Sims and Meera Sitharam and David Urizar},\n      journal= {arXiv preprint arXiv:2403.16145},\n      year   = {2026}\n    }\n\n### Comments\n\n22 pages, 8 figures, 3 tables\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Urizar_D in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AUrizar_D+in%3Amath)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Mar 26 2024 math.CO math.MG arXiv:2403.16145v2 ... @misc{2403.16145, author = {Sean Dewar and Georg Grasegger and Anthony Nixon and Zvi Rosen and William Sims and Meera Sitharam and David Urizar}, title = {{A}ngular constraints on planar frameworks}, year = {2024}, eprint = {2403.16145}, note = {arXiv:2403.16145v2} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... We formulate the problem in matricial terms for cert